    "question": {
      ":question/id": "036a544e-adcf-5604-8565-d9b2d689152b",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$, if $4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n2k=-8\\,⟹\\,k=-4.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
